  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe surface trough won't budge much today, and we'll see \ncoverage of showers increase again through the day. An upper \ntrough will approach from the west and begin to slow down and \nclose off into a low. The broad nature of the forcing with \ndiffluent mid- level flow and the inverted trough is giving \nTuesday more of a scattered shower type look rather than a more \ncoherent shield of rain. PWAT values come down a touch more and \nthe column dries a bit in the mid and upper levels, but showers \nwill still be productive with the bulk of the moisture in the \nwarm cloud layer. Still not concerned about hourly rates as the \n10 km neighborhood probability for 1\"/hour in any given hour is \n20% or less. 24 hour mean totals from HREF through 8am Wednesday\nrange from a quarter to three quarters of an inch, and even the\n90th percentile peaks around 1.25-1.5 inches south of \nPittsburgh. While the flash flooding threat overall may be \ndecreasing especially after tonight, overflow/areal flooding \nthreats remain. Thus, the Flood Watch continues through 12Z \nWednesday. Eastern Ohio, northern West Virginia panhandle, and \nmost of the southwestern Pennsylvania counties were dropped as \nthese areas are expected to avoid the higher totals.\n\nSupport for rain continues through Wednesday and into Thursday as \nthe upper low develops with highest coverage shifting west into \neastern Ohio in tandem with reorientation of the surface trough and \nPVA around the closing upper low. Guidance actually favors coverage \ndecreasing into the afternoon and evening hours which makes sense as \nwe become more centered beneath the upper low and lose the forcing \nlocally, all while surface high pressure over New England begins its \nattempt to nudge southwest. We won't lose the support for continued \nrain until likely Thursday when the upper low exits and the surface \nhigh better establishes and kicks the surface boundary out. Longer \nrange ensembles then point toward a dry weekend locally as a coastal \nlow develops off the Mid-Atlantic and shortwave ridging between that \nand a trough across the Dakotas becomes our dominant feature.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBeneath the cold upper low, with persistent easterly flow, and \nbeneath thick cloud cover and showers, NBM advertises a 60-70% \nchance that our I-80 areas and down the PA & WV ridges can't get\nabove 60F for highs on Tuesday, and similar probabilities \nremain in the ridges on Wednesday. Elsewhere, the goal posts \nspread from low 60s on the cool end to mid 60s on the warm end, \nand given the conditions in place, it's likely a better bet to \nhedge cooler. Minimum temperatures will remain fairly close to \nnormal given abundant moisture, but daytime highs will run 10 \ndegrees below normal through Thursday. So, even optimistically, \nmid 60s is likely our ceiling through Thursday before more \nseasonable temperatures return for the weekend.",
